1
00:00:00,299 --> 00:00:03,220
Nobody joins a cult, not on purpose, not

2
00:00:03,279 --> 00:00:06,139
on day one. They join a community, a

3
00:00:06,279 --> 00:00:09,699
class, a cause, something small, something

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:12,420
reasonable. And by the time the demands turn

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,939
extreme, the person who would have walked away

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,139
on day one no longer exists. This is

7
00:00:18,219 --> 00:00:20,899
why intelligence has almost nothing to do with

8
00:00:20,979 --> 00:00:24,219
who falls for this. This is Shadow Figure.

9
00:00:24,624 --> 00:00:27,134
Here's the part almost everyone gets wrong about

10
00:00:27,224 --> 00:00:30,684
cults. They imagine the recruitment pitch starts

11
00:00:30,724 --> 00:00:33,424
with the crazy part. Give up your family.

12
00:00:34,024 --> 00:00:37,124
Hand over your savings. Obey without question.

13
00:00:37,774 --> 00:00:39,544
No one signs up for that on day

14
00:00:39,624 --> 00:00:42,624
one, and every group that has ever successfully

15
00:00:42,644 --> 00:00:45,544
built that kind of control knows it. If

16
00:00:45,584 --> 00:00:47,954
you asked most people whether they'd hand a

17
00:00:48,084 --> 00:00:51,134
stranger their life savings, cut off contact with

18
00:00:51,164 --> 00:00:54,024
their parents, or stand by while a leader

19
00:00:54,364 --> 00:00:57,864
made increasingly bizarre demands, almost everyone would

20
00:00:57,924 --> 00:01:01,174
say no. Obviously not. They'd see it coming,

21
00:01:01,684 --> 00:01:03,964
and they're right. They would see it coming

22
00:01:04,484 --> 00:01:06,404
if it were presented to them as a

23
00:01:06,464 --> 00:01:09,524
single decision. It never is. It's

24
00:01:09,544 --> 00:01:13,004
presented as dozens of decisions spread across months

25
00:01:13,044 --> 00:01:16,364
or years, each one only slightly bigger than

26
00:01:16,384 --> 00:01:19,384
the last. What actually happens is smaller

27
00:01:19,944 --> 00:01:22,884
and far more reasonable sounding. Come to one

28
00:01:22,984 --> 00:01:25,474
talk. Try one meditation session.

29
00:01:26,244 --> 00:01:29,604
Accept one gift, one invitation, one

30
00:01:29,704 --> 00:01:33,184
act of overwhelming warmth from strangers who seem

31
00:01:33,204 --> 00:01:35,684
to genuinely care about you in a way

32
00:01:35,744 --> 00:01:38,884
your ordinary life doesn't. None of that asks

33
00:01:38,924 --> 00:01:42,344
for much. That's exactly the point. Once a

34
00:01:42,384 --> 00:01:45,844
person says yes to something small, a psychological

35
00:01:45,854 --> 00:01:48,344
pressure kicks in that has nothing to do

36
00:01:48,384 --> 00:01:51,024
with the group itself, the pressure to stay

37
00:01:51,064 --> 00:01:54,364
consistent with your own past decisions. Having

38
00:01:54,444 --> 00:01:57,344
attended the first meeting, agreeing to the second

39
00:01:57,424 --> 00:02:00,744
one feels like simply continuing something you already

40
00:02:00,864 --> 00:02:04,084
chose, not starting something new. Each

41
00:02:04,164 --> 00:02:06,764
step is only slightly larger than the last,

42
00:02:07,224 --> 00:02:09,684
and each one is measured against the step

43
00:02:09,864 --> 00:02:13,104
immediately before it, never against where you started.

44
00:02:13,774 --> 00:02:16,284
That's how a person ends up somewhere they

45
00:02:16,324 --> 00:02:18,794
would have found absurd if you'd described it

46
00:02:18,824 --> 00:02:21,344
to them on day one. They never saw

47
00:02:21,384 --> 00:02:24,264
the whole staircase, only ever the next step,

48
00:02:24,764 --> 00:02:27,544
each one a small, logical-feeling

49
00:02:27,584 --> 00:02:31,004
continuation of the last. Psychologists call the

50
00:02:31,064 --> 00:02:34,484
underlying principle commitment and consistency.

51
00:02:35,044 --> 00:02:38,004
Once we've made a choice, especially a public

52
00:02:38,224 --> 00:02:41,484
or effortful one, we feel internal pressure to

53
00:02:41,504 --> 00:02:44,424
justify it to ourselves as much as to

54
00:02:44,484 --> 00:02:47,504
anyone watching. Saying yes to a small

55
00:02:47,624 --> 00:02:49,924
ask doesn't just open the door to a

56
00:02:49,944 --> 00:02:53,124
bigger one later. It quietly rewrites how the

57
00:02:53,204 --> 00:02:56,264
person sees themselves. I'm the kind of person

58
00:02:56,304 --> 00:02:58,824
who's part of this, and from that point

59
00:02:58,904 --> 00:03:01,797
on, backing out doesn't just mean leaving a

60
00:03:01,856 --> 00:03:04,697
group, it means admitting the earlier version of

61
00:03:04,757 --> 00:03:07,976
yourself was wrong. Most people would rather keep

62
00:03:08,056 --> 00:03:10,316
walking forward than make that admission.

63
00:03:11,337 --> 00:03:13,696
Layered on top of that is something even

64
00:03:13,757 --> 00:03:16,757
more basic, the human need to belong to

65
00:03:16,856 --> 00:03:19,837
something larger than yourself, to a community,

66
00:03:20,376 --> 00:03:22,996
a mission, a family that chose you back.

67
00:03:23,816 --> 00:03:26,736
High control groups are almost without exception

68
00:03:27,156 --> 00:03:30,396
extremely good at manufacturing that feeling faster

69
00:03:30,816 --> 00:03:33,616
and more intensely than ordinary life does.

70
00:03:34,276 --> 00:03:36,996
That feeling isn't fake to the person experiencing

71
00:03:37,056 --> 00:03:39,636
it. It's completely real. What's

72
00:03:39,716 --> 00:03:42,956
manufactured is the specific structure built underneath

73
00:03:43,017 --> 00:03:45,837
it, designed to make leaving cost more than

74
00:03:45,896 --> 00:03:48,956
staying every single week. This

75
00:03:49,017 --> 00:03:52,376
particular lever, consistency, is only one of

76
00:03:52,456 --> 00:03:55,776
several universal principles of influence that show up

77
00:03:55,856 --> 00:03:58,996
again and again across cults, con artists,

78
00:03:59,316 --> 00:04:02,296
and salespeople alike, and we'll spend an entire

79
00:04:02,377 --> 00:04:05,197
future episode unpacking the full set of them.

80
00:04:05,756 --> 00:04:09,036
For now, what matters is this, consistency

81
00:04:09,096 --> 00:04:11,557
pressure alone is strong enough to move a

82
00:04:11,636 --> 00:04:14,496
rational, intelligent adult step by

83
00:04:14,557 --> 00:04:17,396
step into a place they would have rejected

84
00:04:17,476 --> 00:04:20,596
outright if you'd shown them the destination on

85
00:04:20,636 --> 00:04:23,255
day one. We covered this in detail in

86
00:04:23,275 --> 00:04:25,995
an earlier episode, but it's worth returning to

87
00:04:26,055 --> 00:04:28,635
here because it shows the mechanism in its

88
00:04:28,675 --> 00:04:32,145
purest form. Jim Jones didn't open the People's

89
00:04:32,235 --> 00:04:35,075
Temple with talk of mass suicide. He opened

90
00:04:35,115 --> 00:04:38,365
it with genuine food programs, integrated housing

91
00:04:38,575 --> 00:04:41,915
in an era of American segregation, medical care

92
00:04:41,955 --> 00:04:45,415
for people who had none, real help delivered

93
00:04:45,455 --> 00:04:47,575
by a man who seemed to actually mean

94
00:04:47,635 --> 00:04:51,095
it. The demands escalated only after trust was

95
00:04:51,155 --> 00:04:54,175
already built and reinforced by an entire

96
00:04:54,235 --> 00:04:57,155
community of people making the same gradual

97
00:04:57,195 --> 00:05:00,525
commitments together. Loyalty tests got slightly

98
00:05:00,525 --> 00:05:03,745
larger. Contact with the outside world got slightly

99
00:05:03,855 --> 00:05:07,045
smaller. By the time armed guards enforced

100
00:05:07,175 --> 00:05:09,905
isolation in a jungle compound entered the

101
00:05:09,915 --> 00:05:12,555
picture, the members evaluating that

102
00:05:12,595 --> 00:05:15,495
reality weren't comparing it to ordinary life

103
00:05:15,555 --> 00:05:18,515
anymore. They were comparing it to last month

104
00:05:18,555 --> 00:05:21,435
inside the group, which had already normalized so

105
00:05:21,555 --> 00:05:24,675
much. Against that baseline, each new

106
00:05:24,695 --> 00:05:28,115
demand looked like a small, almost logical next

107
00:05:28,195 --> 00:05:31,415
step. What makes this example so instructive

108
00:05:31,895 --> 00:05:34,675
is that many of Jonestown's members were not

109
00:05:34,715 --> 00:05:37,755
naive newcomers to the world. They included

110
00:05:37,815 --> 00:05:40,895
teachers, nurses, and people who had spent

111
00:05:40,975 --> 00:05:44,435
years actively fighting for civil rights and social

112
00:05:44,495 --> 00:05:47,475
justice elsewhere. They weren't drawn in by

113
00:05:47,495 --> 00:05:50,675
stupidity. They were drawn in by a genuinely

114
00:05:50,685 --> 00:05:53,795
appealing early mission delivered by someone who,

115
00:05:54,155 --> 00:05:56,695
for years, appeared to be exactly who he

116
00:05:56,755 --> 00:05:59,815
claimed to be. The control only fully revealed

117
00:05:59,855 --> 00:06:03,005
itself after leaving no longer felt like a

118
00:06:03,055 --> 00:06:06,355
simple decision because by then, leaving

119
00:06:06,375 --> 00:06:09,855
meant abandoning the community, the identity,

120
00:06:09,865 --> 00:06:12,675
and in many cases, the family members who

121
00:06:12,735 --> 00:06:15,765
had joined alongside them. By the time members

122
00:06:15,795 --> 00:06:18,995
were living in a remote jungle compound thousands

123
00:06:19,035 --> 00:06:22,235
of miles from anything familiar with passports

124
00:06:22,335 --> 00:06:25,755
confiscated and outside mail read before it reached

125
00:06:25,795 --> 00:06:28,975
them, the version of normal they were measuring

126
00:06:28,985 --> 00:06:31,815
their daily life against had already shifted

127
00:06:31,855 --> 00:06:34,675
enormously from where it stood years earlier.

128
00:06:35,425 --> 00:06:38,225
That's not a failure of character. That's what

129
00:06:38,235 --> 00:06:41,445
happens to anyone's baseline for normal when it's

130
00:06:41,515 --> 00:06:43,755
allowed to move an inch at a time

131
00:06:43,835 --> 00:06:46,875
for years without ever being checked against where

132
00:06:46,935 --> 00:06:50,413
it started. The second mechanism running underneath

133
00:06:50,473 --> 00:06:53,173
the first one is what researchers and former

134
00:06:53,273 --> 00:06:56,153
members alike have come to call love bombing,

135
00:06:56,873 --> 00:06:59,453
a sudden overwhelming wave of attention,

136
00:06:59,833 --> 00:07:03,253
affection, and belonging directed at a new

137
00:07:03,293 --> 00:07:06,413
or prospective member, often within the first few

138
00:07:06,473 --> 00:07:09,833
encounters. Most people go their entire adult

139
00:07:09,913 --> 00:07:12,753
lives without ever being made to feel as

140
00:07:12,813 --> 00:07:16,233
instantly, completely welcomed as high control

141
00:07:16,333 --> 00:07:18,933
groups make a newcomer feel in a single

142
00:07:19,033 --> 00:07:22,493
afternoon. That feeling is manufactured, but

143
00:07:22,513 --> 00:07:25,793
it doesn't feel manufactured from the inside. It

144
00:07:25,833 --> 00:07:28,973
feels like finally being seen. And once that

145
00:07:29,013 --> 00:07:31,593
emotional hook is set, it becomes the thing

146
00:07:31,813 --> 00:07:34,293
a person doesn't want to lose long before

147
00:07:34,353 --> 00:07:36,853
they've had time to evaluate what else comes

148
00:07:36,893 --> 00:07:40,073
attached to it. Running parallel to this is

149
00:07:40,133 --> 00:07:43,603
a slow, rarely announced narrowing of a person's

150
00:07:43,693 --> 00:07:47,013
outside relationships. More time at the group

151
00:07:47,073 --> 00:07:49,813
means less time with the friends and family

152
00:07:49,823 --> 00:07:51,873
who might ask uncomfortable questions.

153
00:07:52,753 --> 00:07:55,243
Eventually, the group isn't just a part of

154
00:07:55,273 --> 00:07:58,333
someone's life, it's the only remaining source

155
00:07:58,793 --> 00:08:01,343
of the specific kind of validation and

156
00:08:01,413 --> 00:08:04,833
belonging they've come to depend on. At that

157
00:08:04,873 --> 00:08:08,283
point, the people best positioned to say, "This

158
00:08:08,313 --> 00:08:11,403
looks wrong," have already been quietly filtered out

159
00:08:11,433 --> 00:08:13,783
of the person's daily life. This

160
00:08:13,853 --> 00:08:17,093
isolation rarely arrives as a rule.

161
00:08:17,153 --> 00:08:19,513
Nobody is usually told directly to cut off

162
00:08:19,523 --> 00:08:22,153
their family. It happens through scheduling,

163
00:08:22,813 --> 00:08:25,833
every evening and weekend gradually filled with group

164
00:08:25,913 --> 00:08:29,413
activities, every outside relationship quietly

165
00:08:29,513 --> 00:08:32,893
framed as slightly less enlightened, less committed,

166
00:08:33,433 --> 00:08:36,653
less understanding of what we're building here than

167
00:08:36,713 --> 00:08:39,913
the people inside. Former members of high control

168
00:08:39,993 --> 00:08:43,373
groups consistently describe the same arc, not

169
00:08:43,453 --> 00:08:45,793
a single dramatic break from their old life,

170
00:08:46,293 --> 00:08:48,873
but a slow erosion of it. One skipped

171
00:08:48,933 --> 00:08:51,513
phone call and one missed family dinner at

172
00:08:51,553 --> 00:08:54,793
a time until one day the group is

173
00:08:54,833 --> 00:08:57,393
the only phone number that still feels like

174
00:08:57,433 --> 00:09:00,840
home. There's a third mechanism worth naming on

175
00:09:00,901 --> 00:09:03,141
its own because it's the one that makes

176
00:09:03,201 --> 00:09:05,780
a group nearly impossible to argue with from

177
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:09,240
the inside. The leader positions themselves as the

178
00:09:09,420 --> 00:09:12,680
sole valid interpreter of reality so that any

179
00:09:12,820 --> 00:09:15,961
doubt a member feels gets redirected

180
00:09:16,020 --> 00:09:19,461
inward at themselves instead of outward

181
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:23,120
at the group. Researchers and former members who

182
00:09:23,201 --> 00:09:26,280
study high control groups have described this as

183
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:29,520
a kind of closed information loop. Every source

184
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:32,640
of truth, from scripture to daily instruction,

185
00:09:33,120 --> 00:09:35,820
runs through one person or one small inner

186
00:09:35,881 --> 00:09:39,180
circle, and any outside information that contradicts

187
00:09:39,241 --> 00:09:42,600
it is pre-labeled in advance as a test

188
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:45,881
of faith, an attack from enemies, or evidence

189
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:48,680
that the doubting member simply hasn't understood deeply

190
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:51,920
enough yet. Notice what that structure accomplishes.

191
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:55,620
It doesn't just answer objections. It removes the

192
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:58,420
possibility of a valid objection existing at all.

193
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:01,620
This is why members who leave high control

194
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:04,780
groups so often describe a specific

195
00:10:04,881 --> 00:10:08,020
disorienting symptom afterward. Not just

196
00:10:08,101 --> 00:10:10,840
grief, but a kind of vertigo about their

197
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:13,940
own judgment because for months or years,

198
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:17,680
every internal alarm bell they felt was actively

199
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:21,000
reinterpreted by the group's own logic as

200
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:23,540
proof they needed to trust the group more,

201
00:10:23,601 --> 00:10:26,474
not less. None of this is about being

202
00:10:26,514 --> 00:10:30,004
gullible. It's about a set of mechanisms that

203
00:10:30,054 --> 00:10:33,284
work on the specific architecture of human social

204
00:10:33,294 --> 00:10:35,994
psychology, the same architecture

205
00:10:36,354 --> 00:10:39,414
responsible for ordinary trust, loyalty,

206
00:10:39,774 --> 00:10:43,004
and belonging in healthy relationships. The

207
00:10:43,054 --> 00:10:46,314
manipulation isn't a separate exotic thing.

208
00:10:47,254 --> 00:10:50,354
It's a hijacking of completely normal wiring.

209
00:10:51,114 --> 00:10:54,394
Two habits protect you better than raw intelligence

210
00:10:54,574 --> 00:10:57,554
ever will. First, notice the total,

211
00:10:58,014 --> 00:11:00,554
not just the step. Before agreeing to the

212
00:11:00,574 --> 00:11:03,274
next small ask from any group, cause, or

213
00:11:03,294 --> 00:11:06,694
relationship, mentally compare it to where you started

214
00:11:06,754 --> 00:11:09,864
months ago, not to yesterday. If you wouldn't

215
00:11:09,914 --> 00:11:12,214
have agreed to your current situation on day

216
00:11:12,254 --> 00:11:15,714
one, that gap is information, even if each

217
00:11:15,774 --> 00:11:18,394
individual step felt reasonable at the time.

218
00:11:19,314 --> 00:11:22,174
Second, protect at least one relationship

219
00:11:22,474 --> 00:11:24,954
outside any group that asks for your total

220
00:11:24,964 --> 00:11:28,344
time and loyalty, a friend, a family member,

221
00:11:28,954 --> 00:11:31,264
anyone with no stake in the group's continued

222
00:11:31,334 --> 00:11:34,824
existence who you talk to regularly enough that

223
00:11:34,824 --> 00:11:38,004
they'd notice a change in you. Isolation is

224
00:11:38,054 --> 00:11:40,874
never announced as a rule. It's the byproduct

225
00:11:40,954 --> 00:11:43,614
of a hundred small choices to spend time

226
00:11:43,694 --> 00:11:47,034
inside instead of outside. The people who leave

227
00:11:47,054 --> 00:11:50,254
high-control groups earliest are almost always the

228
00:11:50,294 --> 00:11:52,834
ones who kept at least one honest outside

229
00:11:52,914 --> 00:11:55,004
voice in their life the whole way through.

230
00:11:55,714 --> 00:11:59,034
A third quieter habit, pay attention to how

231
00:11:59,174 --> 00:12:01,734
a group or a person responds when you

232
00:12:01,854 --> 00:12:05,014
ask a direct question about money, structure, or

233
00:12:05,054 --> 00:12:08,184
leadership. Not whether they answer confidently,

234
00:12:08,754 --> 00:12:11,134
but whether they answer the actual question you

235
00:12:11,254 --> 00:12:14,594
asked. Deflection dressed up as depth, "You're

236
00:12:14,614 --> 00:12:17,114
not ready to understand that yet. That's a

237
00:12:17,214 --> 00:12:19,914
question that will make sense later," is one

238
00:12:19,974 --> 00:12:23,094
of the most consistent early signals across every

239
00:12:23,154 --> 00:12:26,464
high-control group ever documented long before

240
00:12:26,534 --> 00:12:29,244
any of the more extreme demands appear. And

241
00:12:29,274 --> 00:12:32,494
finally, give yourself explicit permission in

242
00:12:32,554 --> 00:12:35,653
advance to change your mind later without that

243
00:12:35,734 --> 00:12:38,394
meaning you were foolish now. The fear of

244
00:12:38,434 --> 00:12:41,574
admitting a past decision was wrong is precisely

245
00:12:41,974 --> 00:12:45,034
the lever this entire mechanism runs on. A

246
00:12:45,054 --> 00:12:47,914
person who has already decided ahead of

247
00:12:47,974 --> 00:12:51,334
time that leaving a group or a relationship

248
00:12:51,414 --> 00:12:54,294
is not an admission of stupidity but simply

249
00:12:54,334 --> 00:12:57,594
new information changing an old decision has

250
00:12:57,654 --> 00:13:00,414
quietly disarmed the single most powerful

251
00:13:00,434 --> 00:13:03,314
psychological trap in this entire episode.

252
00:13:03,932 --> 00:13:06,812
The people who joined Jonestown, who joined every

253
00:13:06,932 --> 00:13:09,872
cult you've ever read about, were not unusually

254
00:13:10,012 --> 00:13:13,133
foolish. Many were educated, thoughtful,

255
00:13:13,433 --> 00:13:16,772
and deliberate. What they lacked wasn't intelligence.

256
00:13:17,252 --> 00:13:19,693
It was a specific kind of awareness of

257
00:13:19,752 --> 00:13:23,052
how gradual, consistent, small commitments

258
00:13:23,412 --> 00:13:26,673
compound into something no reasonable person would have

259
00:13:26,752 --> 00:13:29,912
agreed to upfront. None of the four mechanisms

260
00:13:29,992 --> 00:13:32,612
we covered today—consistency pressure,

261
00:13:33,112 --> 00:13:36,272
manufactured belonging, isolation, and the

262
00:13:36,373 --> 00:13:39,612
